Ray <bear@sonic.net> wrote:
>
> I tend to agree.  Nethack has fulfilled the potential its basic design and 
> data structures made possible.  

I don't think that somebody thought "let's see how far we can get if
we structure the internal data this way".

> Fundamental advances in the game, at this point, would require a different 
> underlying architecture, design, and data structures.

Why is that a problem? Architecture and data structures can be
changed.

But somebody has to do it. But that somebody isn't really motivated if
the changes aren't likely to be taken up by the DevTeam or any
developer at all. Just distributing your changes as patches won't get
them played by a lot of people.

There is somebody who has rewritten some vital part of the NetHack
code: Pasi Kallinen is doing a terrific job in changing NetHack's
level compiler in SporkHack.

> It's reached the
> point where the only things you can really change, without ripping a bunch 
> of stuff out and rewriting it according to a radically different software
> design, are trivial details.  

That's not true. Even if you leave aside game play changes there is
still a lot of improvement possible.

Vanilla NetHack doesn't have menucolors, hpmon/statuscolors or the
dump patch. Just to name the three most popular patches.

Jukka Lahtinen's combined patch and NAO's patch are both about 190K.
That's a lot more than trivial details (Please, don't tell me that
interface improvements are trivial details. If you do, I will tell
Jakob Nielsen and he will come to hunt you in your dreams).

> There's definitely a bugfix release to be made.

As far as we know that won't happen. 3.4.1, 3.4.2 and 3.4.3 was a
bug-fix release and the buglist continued to grow at steady pace.

If they would have wanted to release a 3.4.4 they could have done that
years ago. In 2003 we saw 3 bugfix releases, afterwards none.

Worse, if you nowadays report a bug you usually get the answer that's
"fixed" or "doesn't happen" in the "development version". I think it's
safe to say that the DevTeam doesn't have a version branch named 3.4.4
on their drives.

> But beyond that, minor 
> reshuffling of details is all that's possible within the identity of the 
> current game.  If enough basic architecture to change something major 
> gets changed, the game won't be nethack anymore.  

I'm sorry to say that, but that's a stupid conclusion.

It's not up to us to define what "NetHack" is.

Every game that gets released by the DevTeam that bears this name *is*
"NetHack". We might not like the changes of this hypothetical release
but that doesn't change that fact.

> People who think Nethack needs a new release are invited to play Rogue.

You don't even have to go back to Rogue to have an unmistakably
different game.

Hack 1.0 until 1.0.3 were much more like Rogue than NetHack. The
versions before NetHack 3.0.0 had much less items and monsters and
e.g. no form of astral plane. The branched dungeon only appeared in
3.1.0. The wizard patch (the modern form of magic in NetHack) and
Sokoban got added in 3.3.0.

